      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the greatest possible value of \\( x \\) such that \\( x \\) is a positive multiple of 3 and \\( x^3 < 1000 \\), we can start by estimating the cube root of 1000.\n\nFirst, calculate the cube root of 1000:\n\\[\n\\sqrt[3]{1000} = 10\n\\]\nSince \\( x \\) must be a positive multiple of 3 and \\( x^3 < 1000 \\), we need to find the largest multiple of 3 that is less than the cube root of 1000.\n\nNext, we list some multiples of 3:\n\\[\n3, 6, 9, 12, 15, \\ldots\n\\]\nWe check each one to ensure it meets \\( x^3 < 1000 \\):\n\\[\n3^3 = 27 \\quad \\text{(which is less than 1000)}\n\\]\n\\[\n6^3 = 216 \\quad \\text{(which is less than 1000)}\n\\]\n\\[\n9^3 = 729 \\quad \\text{(which is less than 1000)}\n\\]\n\\[\n15^3 = 3375 \\quad \\text{(which is greater than 1000)}\n\\]\n\nAmong these, the largest multiple of 3 where the cube is still less than 1000 is 9. Therefore, the greatest possible value of \\( x \\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{9}\n\\]",
